        }
      },
      {
        "id": "ex-known-variance-normal-z-sample-size-and-sensitivity",
        "kind": "example",
        "title": "An exact one-sided normal sample size and its effect-variance sensitivity",
        "statement": "Take any \\(\\sigma>0\\), set \\(\\delta=\\sigma\\), and choose \\(\\alpha=\\beta_{\\rm II}=1-\\Phi(1)\\). For the upper-tail test \\(H_0:\\mu\\le\\mu_0\\) against \\(\\mu_1=\\mu_0+\\delta\\), the exact planning rule gives least sample size \\(n_*=4\\) for target power \\(1-\\beta_{\\rm II}\\). At \\(n=4\\), halving the target effect with the same \\(\\sigma\\) lowers power to \\(1/2\\). Keeping \\(\\delta=\\sigma\\) and \\(n=4\\) but doubling the standard deviation (quadrupling the variance) also lowers power to \\(1/2\\).",
        "strategy": "direct",
        "deps": [
          "def-axiom-of-choice",
